    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#gian-galeazzo-visconti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti.jpg" alt="Gian Galeazzo Visconti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GiovAmbrogiodePredisattribGianGaleazzoVisconti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Giovanni Ambrogio de Predis, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#milan">Milan</a> grew out of the signoria, or lordship, that the Visconti family had built over Milan and the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lombardy">Lombardy</a> since the late thirteenth century. In 1385 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> seized sole power by arresting and imprisoning his uncle Bernabò, and in 1395 he purchased the title of duke from the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-empire">Romans</a>, Wenceslas, for a large sum, reportedly 100,000 florins. The investiture turned a personal lordship into a hereditary imperial fief and gave the Visconti a legitimacy that the communal traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> cities had long denied them. Gian Galeazzo used the duchy as the base for the most ambitious expansion in late medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>. He had already taken Verona and Vicenza in 1387 and Padua in 1388; he then acquired Pisa in 1399, received the submission of Siena and Perugia in 1399–1400 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bologna">Bologna</a> in 1402. By the summer of 1402 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#florence">Florence</a> seemed isolated, but Gian Galeazzo died of plague in September of that year, and his state broke apart in the hands of his young sons. Its core in Lombardy, with Milan and Pavia, nonetheless survived and remained one of the largest and richest states of the Italian peninsula.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#milan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milan.jpg" alt="Milan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#milan">Milan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milan_Cathedral_from_Piazza_del_Duomo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jiuguang Wang, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#milan">Milan</a> was one of the largest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, with probably some 100,000 inhabitants in the fifteenth century, set in the irrigated plain of the Po valley. Canals such as the Naviglio Grande carried water and goods, and Cistercian and lay landowners turned the lowlands into meadows, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> fields and dairy farms; rice cultivation spread in the later fifteenth century. The city’s armourers were famous across Europe, supplying helmets and plate armour to princes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenary</a> captains, while wool, fustian and above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a>, promoted by the Sforza, employed thousands of workers. Merchants and bankers traded through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genoa">Genoa</a> and across the Alpine passes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a>. Religious life centred on the Ambrosian rite, unique to the archdiocese, and on great building projects: Gian Galeazzo began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#milan-cathedral">Milan Cathedral</a> in 1386 and the Certosa di Pavia in 1396, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a> founded the Ospedale Maggiore, designed by Filarete, in 1456. Under Spanish rule the economy suffered from heavy taxation and the plague of 1630, while the Counter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> archbishop Charles Borromeo reshaped parish life. Austrian rule in the eighteenth century brought a land survey, the Theresian cadastre, completed in 1760, and renewed agricultural prosperity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pavia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia.jpg" alt="Battle of Pavia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Pavia,_1525_(by_Rupert_Heller)_-_Nationalmuseum,_Stockholm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rupert Heller, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Visconti and Sforza relied on condottieri, professional captains who hired out their companies under contract, and on the wealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lombardy">Lombardy</a> to pay them. Under Filippo Maria Visconti (r. 1412–1447) captains such as Francesco Bussone da Carmagnola and Niccolò Piccinino reconquered much of Lombardy, but Carmagnola later served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#venice">Venice</a> and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#milan">Milan</a> at the Battle of Maclodio in 1427; Venice gained Brescia and Bergamo, and its frontier stayed on the Adda River for centuries. Milan ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genoa">Genoa</a> from 1421 to 1435. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, himself the most successful condottiere of his age, won the duchy in 1450 by blockading the city. The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> of 1454 then stabilised Italian rivalries for forty years. The French invasions changed everything: Louis XII, claiming the duchy through his grandmother Valentina Visconti, conquered it in 1499, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a> was captured at Novara in 1500. Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pike">pikemen</a> restored the Sforza in 1512 and won at Novara in 1513, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francis-i">Francis I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> defeated them at the Battle of Marignano in 1515. Imperial forces drove out the French, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a> in 1525, fought near the duchy’s second city, ended with Francis I a prisoner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#francesco-sforza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza.jpg" alt="Francesco Sforza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francesco_Sforza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">School of Lombardy, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Visconti and Sforza dukes ruled through a privy council and a council of justice, a chancery staffed by trained secretaries and a network of podestà and commissioners in the subject cities, which kept their own statutes and councils. Historians such as Jane Black have stressed that the dukes claimed a ‘plenitude of power’ that allowed them to override local law, though in practice they negotiated with urban elites and feudatories. Milanese ambassadors resident at foreign courts made the duchy a pioneer of permanent diplomacy. After the death of Filippo Maria in 1447 without a legitimate son, citizens proclaimed the Golden Ambrosian Republic, but it collapsed in 1450 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, husband of Filippo Maria’s daughter Bianca Maria, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#milan">Milan</a> as duke. His son Galeazzo Maria was assassinated in 1476; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a>, known as il Moro, governed as regent for his nephew from 1480 and as duke from 1494. Louis XII created the Senate of Milan in 1499, a supreme court that survived until 1786. After the last Sforza duke, Francesco II, died in 1535, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> kept the duchy and invested his son Philip; Spanish governors, the Senate and the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madrid">Madrid</a> ruled it until 1706.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ludovico-sforza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza.jpg" alt="Ludovico Sforza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pala_Sforzesca_-_detail_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown Master, Italian (active 1490-1520 in Lombardy), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#milan">Milan</a> was one of the great centres of the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Renaissance</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a>’s court employed Leonardo da Vinci from about 1482 to 1499; there he painted the <em>Last Supper</em> in the refectory of Santa Maria delle Grazie, c. 1495–1498, designed a colossal equestrian monument to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a> and filled notebooks with engineering and anatomical studies. Donato Bramante built the tribune of Santa Maria delle Grazie, and the University of Pavia, founded under the Visconti in 1361, trained lawyers and physicians for the duchy. The Visconti library at Pavia was among the finest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. Politically, the Milanese claim of the house of Orléans drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> and made the duchy the cockpit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>, while the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> and the Italic League of 1455 offered an early model of a balance of power. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>, Milan was the hinge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> strategy, the starting point of the Spanish Road that took troops north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a>. In the eighteenth century Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lombardy">Lombardy</a> became a centre of the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>: Pietro Verri’s journal <em>Il Caffè</em> and Cesare Beccaria’s <em>On Crimes and Punishments</em> (1764) influenced penal reform across Europe, and the Teatro alla Scala opened in 1778.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-wars.jpg" alt="Italian Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars">Italian Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italian_War_Battle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Waylon1104, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The duchy lost its independence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. After 1535 it became a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, confirmed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> by the Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis in 1559, and its revenues and soldiers served wars fought elsewhere. Heavy taxation, troop billeting and the plague of 1630, which killed perhaps half the population of the city and is the setting of Alessandro Manzoni’s novel <em>The Betrothed</em>, weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lombardy">Lombardy</a> in the seventeenth century. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> Austrian troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#milan">Milan</a> in 1706, and the Treaty of Rastatt in 1714 confirmed Austrian rule. Savoy, a wartime ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austria">Austria</a>, obtained Alessandria, Novara, Tortona and other western districts in stages between 1707 and 1748. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> and Joseph II reforming ministers modernised taxation and administration, and in 1786 the old Senate was abolished. The end came with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>: after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>’s victory at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> he entered Milan on 15 May 1796. The Transpadane Republic was set up in November 1796 and merged into the Cisalpine Republic in 1797, and Austria renounced Lombardy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> that October. After 1815 Lombardy was ruled by Austria as part of the Kingdom of Lombardy–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#venetia">Venetia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#milan">Milan</a> was the richest state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Renaissance</a> northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>, where the Visconti and Sforza built a strong court and bureaucracy and Leonardo painted the Last Supper, and whose disputed succession made it the main prize of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> and later a pillar of Spanish and Austrian power in Italy.</em></p>

